    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_9eb83b25a2929aa3bec8a167da1e622e51bf988683076672a06a78e5f0cd576c",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source-grounded mathematics: f_z(t)=1/(z−t) lies in H^{1/2} off R because its Fourier transform is one-sided and exponentially decaying. Smooth cutoffs converge in H^{1/2} and identify the centered statistic with ξ_∞'/ξ_∞ minus the stated half-plane constant. The blue-noise theorem then gives finite-dimensional convergence of the dilated centered resolvent to a Gaussian field with explicit covariance. Continuity plus an L² vanishing-contour calculation and a countable rational-polygon reduction produce a holomorphic version; increment bounds uniform in the dilation parameter give tightness and hence compact-open convergence in law. Conceptual interpretation: the resolvent test family turns distribution-indexed blue noise into an analytic random function, but holomorphy and process convergence require covariance/contour structure and tightness beyond pointwise Gaussian limits.",
