    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_fbd779ceb9381b6a13a6d6d96ec24994516ba28b68ba6469c9cf93ba1ca1761a",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source-grounded mathematics: for finite n and the limit, distant zeros are partitioned into polynomially growing positive/negative spatial blocks. Reciprocal terms are approximated by signed block scales, separating centered signed-count cancellation from smaller absolute-count errors. Jensen's inequality with probability weights and determinantal interval-count exponential moments yields a tail exponential bound uniform in n and z on compact K⊂C\\R, once A dominates the stated p-dependent scale. Conceptual interpretation: blocking preserves cancellation that termwise absolute bounds would discard and converts a global resolvent tail into a weighted family of local count fluctuations. This controls the symmetrically/spatially regularized tail off the real axis; it does not make the raw series absolutely convergent.",
