    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_a41adbf113d5089ec375b07510eb819c1a8a75eec592a17a74cca1fa3037662c",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Polynomially growing spatial blocks preserve cancellation between positive and negative distant-zero counts, allowing determinantal count exponential moments to control the regularized resolvent tail uniformly in finite n and in the limit. The resulting L^p decay licenses removal of spatial cutoffs in logarithmic-derivative moments. Integrating the logarithmic derivative along zero-free off-real paths converts this additive tail control into uniform moments for balanced function ratios, which upgrades almost-sure compact convergence to convergence of their expectations. That probabilistic passage then carries the separately justified finite-CUE Giambelli determinant identity to ξ_∞.",
