    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_dcfba2aec9f8ba8c699f6424048965d6f12800062e217c2be87ddd270d2aa641",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Local sine-process convergence identifies limiting zero configurations but does not determine the normalization or tails of an entire function. The virtual-isometry coupling aligns indexed eigenangles across dimensions; the count-concentration and local-coupling regimes are then patched to obtain y_k^(n)=k+O(log(2+|k|)) uniformly in n and k. Rewriting the normalized characteristic-polynomial ratio as a symmetrically truncated zero product uses that rigidity to turn paired tails into summable O(log k/k²) errors, so finite-product convergence and Montel compactness yield the compact-open entire-function limit.",
